  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 138 AM Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... A slow moving cold front will keep unsettled \nweather in place today, with cloudy, cool, and wet conditions \nexpected. \n\nEarly morning water vapor imagery reveals broad southwesterly flow \naloft, with a series of embedded shortwaves across GA/SC/southern \nNC. At the surface, weak high pressure was located over New England \nwhile a surface cold front stretched from the Coastal Plain through \nthe Sandhills and westward toward KCLT. The front has made slow but \nsteady southward progress since Saturday afternoon although forward \nprogress has slowed greatly this evening. Dewpoints north of the \nfront have fallen into the lower 50s while they remain in the upper \n60s/lower 70s to the south. \n\nThe front, which will become increasingly stationary today, should \nserve as the primary focal point for precip chances through at least \nmid day. With a series of weak upstream shortwaves forecast to move \nthrough the area today in close proximity to the stalled surface \nboundary. precip chances are likely to be maximized south and east \nof the Triangle. While light rain and drizzle are certainly expected \nelsewhere, the greatest precip totals are likely to be confined to \nthe southern 1/3rd of the forecast area. \n\nConditions will slowly dry out from NW to SE late this afternoon \ninto tonight, although most of the 00Z guidance suggests a continued \nperiod of showers across the southern Coastal Plain well into early \nMonday morning. \n\nIn terms of additional precip: expect a tenth to a quarter across \nthe Triad and Triangle, with a half to three quarters of an inch \nfarther south and east.\n\nFinally, the extensive cloud cover and precip will keep temps well \nbelow climatological normals for early October. Highs will only \nreach the mid/upper 60s north of the stalled surface front, while \nmaking a run at the low/mid 70s to the south. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Much drier air a will arrive Monday afternoon and \nTuesday, then last through much of the upcoming week. Temperatures \nwill average close to normal for early October through mid-week, \nthen trend above normal late in the week.\n\nLingering moisture including low stratus, some fog, and even a \nlingering light shower (SE) early Monday will slowly give way to \nmuch drier air arriving from the NW Monday afternoon and Monday \nnight. Expect sunshine to return to the northern Piedmont early \nMonday, with partly sunny skies in the east and south through much \nof the day. Highs will top out in the 70s. It may be warmer in the \nNW Piedmont than in the SE Coastal Plain as the sun will likely be \nslow to return at Goldsboro and Clinton. \n\nRegardless, much drier air associated with surface high pressure \nbuilding from the Great Lakes and Mid-Atlantic states will bring \nmainly clear/sunny conditions by Monday night into Wednesday night. \nLows will dip into the 40s/50s NW to SE Monday night, mostly 40s \nTuesday night, and around 50 Wednesday night. Highs will cool into \nthe upper 60s to mid 70s Tuesday and Wednesday. \n\nReinforcing dry air associated with another high pressure will \nspread into the region Thursday and Friday. The temperatures will \nmost likely trend into the mid 70s to around 80 late week, with lows \nin the 50s. \n\nThe next chance of rain appears to be possibly late next weekend.",
